### 2a. #430 / #436 / #439 — STALE, on the farm's own inputs

1,960 artifacts, 7,840 (artifact, entry point) runs, 0 panics, on the tree the issues
were still open against. Combined with §1 (no post-fix artifact exists) and the
2026-08-14 record's line archaeology (`mc_arm.rs:5930` is the pre-da53bfa position of
the dst-range statement), these three are the stale-build re-files that record
hypothesised. The statement itself still exists (now `mc_arm.rs:6027`, sized
`(h-1)*stride + w` since 5a208de) and is guarded by `tests/crash_vectors/
arm_mc16_overshoot.obu` in `GUARDED`. Nothing in the code was touched for them.

### 2b. #444 — LIVE, fixed on this branch

Both artifacts panic on all four entry points at the 16bpc bilinear `mc_put` SOURCE
range (`mc_arm.rs:6034:61`, the `src_bytes[src_start..src_start + src_byte_len]` in
`mc_put_dispatch_inner`). Root cause, read off the two panic messages:

    src_byte_len = ((h + 1) * stride + w + 1) * 2          (unconditional)

but `put_bilin_16bpc_inner` reads h rows plus one only when my != 0, w columns plus
one only when mx != 0, and on the last row only those columns. A 16bpc plane buffer
ends exactly where its last row's w pixels end (8192 bytes = a 64x64 plane; 32768 =
128x128), so a block in the bottom-right corner overshoots by up to two strides in the
copy case. `mct_prep_dispatch`'s BPC16 Bilinear arm had the identical expression and
the identical kernel read pattern (`prep_bilin_16bpc_inner`); both now use
`bilin_16bpc_src_extent`. The 8bpc arms slice open-ended and were never affected.

    after fix:  #444  2 files, 8 runs, 0 decoded a frame, 0 panicked
    seeds:      fuzz/regression/parse_seq_header/crash-mc16-bilin-src-overshoot-{a,b}
    mutation:   both call sites reverted -> fuzz_regression FAILS, 8 pairs named,
                the two messages above; restored -> ok (3 passed)


## 3. #516 — LIVE by construction, x86_64 only

Signature `mc.rs:5337:56`, farm message (repro.txt):

    range end index 18 out of range for slice of length 17

Line 5337 on `main` is `let a10 = loadu_128!(<&[u16; 8]>::try_from(&src[col + 10..
col + 18]).unwrap())` in `h_filter_8tap_16bpc_put_avx2_inner`, byte-identical to
d26c404 (main on the filing date). The caller hands the kernel an open-ended
`&src[row - 3..]` of the whole plane; 17 pixels left means the row is the last of the
buffer and w = 8 (w + 9). The filter needs pixels col..col+15 (8 outputs x 8 taps,
last tap of output 7 at col + 14); the kernel loaded col+k..col+k+8 for k = 0..11
and consumed only the low 4 u16 of each (lane insert + `_mm256_unpacklo_epi16`), so
it demanded col + 18 for data it never used. Same shape in
`h_filter_8tap_16bpc_avx2_inner` (H+V mid pass) and
`h_filter_8tap_16bpc_prep_direct_avx2_inner`. The AVX-512 variants load exactly
w + 7. Fix: all 36 loads are 64-bit (`loadu_64!`); furthest read col + 14.

What could and could not be run here:

    cargo test  --release --target x86_64-apple-darwin --test fuzz_regression   ok (3 passed)
    cargo clippy --release --target x86_64-apple-darwin --lib ... -D warnings    clean
    crash_sweep (x86_64-apple-darwin) over fuzz/regression + tests/crash_vectors
        + the #516 artifact                                     34 files, 136 runs, 0 panicked

    is_x86_feature_detected!("avx2") under Rosetta 2 on this host:   false
    (sse4.2 true, avx512f false; docker --platform linux/amd64 likewise shows no avx2)

So the AVX2 kernel itself did not execute on this box before or after the change.
Before-evidence is the farm's own run of the artifact against d26c404 (repro.txt);
after-evidence is CI's ubuntu-latest `fuzz_regression` leg, which has AVX2 and now
carries `fuzz/regression/parse_seq_header/crash-mc16-h8tap-avx2-src-overread`.


